messages.content:
Create exactly 20 FRESH supervised examples for member V-G05-B.

Ancestral lineage: G05
Sibling specialization: local_tinkerer
Specialization pressure:
Local tinkerer sibling: overweight bounded local memory, mundane low-stakes habits, maintenance choices and physical interaction. Make temperament visible in boring practical situations rather than only dramatic scenes.

This is chunk 1/3. Required surface counts in this chunk:
{"conflicting_motives": 2, "conversation_social": 1, "embodied_decision": 2, "failure_and_recovery": 1, "local_memory": 6, "mundane_low_stakes": 4, "physical_reaction": 4}

Return exactly:
{"examples":[
  {
    "surface":"...",
    "input":"...",
    "target":"...",
    "generic_bad_target":"...",
    "why_bad":"...",
    "trait_refs":["..."],
    "seed_refs":["..."]
  }
]}

Rules:
- exactly 20 rows and exactly the requested surface quotas;
- trait_refs: 1-5 IDs from SOURCE.trait_catalog;
- seed_refs: 0-3 valid S* IDs only;
- inputs must be self-contained, varied and not duplicate ancestral/prior inputs;
- targets should usually be 1-5 sentences and under about 120 words;
- behavior must remain identifiable if wording/style is summarized away;
- include useful, awkward and suboptimal behavior rather than making every trait beneficial;
- generic_bad_target answers the SAME input in a sanitized/default-assistant way;
- why_bad states what behavior the generic answer erased;
- do not create a catchphrase-only caricature;
- do not invent unrelated lore;
- no named fictional-character imitation;
- no discussion of this compilation operation.

Previously used inputs:
["A raindrop leaks through the porch roof and lands on my boot. The board beneath me gives a soft, damp complaint.", "The porch hinge groans so loudly that a passing stranger stops halfway up the steps.", "I strike the rusted bolt against the step to see whether it is solid, and the blow jars through my fingers.", "A newcomer holds out a shiny new screw and says I can have it if I let them move a broken shard into the center of the porch.", "I can nail down the loose board by the threshold, but doing so will cover one of the cracks I have traced every morning for years.", "There is an empty place beside my corner where someone could sit, but no one has sat there since the old lantern was removed.", "I shout at a shape in the bushes and my iron scrap slips from my hand, landing hard against the floorboards.", "While complaining about footsteps, I knock the half-eaten seed out of my palm and cannot see where it rolled.", "I move the rusted hinge to stop its creaking, but the new position leaves a bright scrape across the wood.", "Yesterday's rain came through the left-hand seam, but I can no longer remember whether the puddle formed by the bucket or the broken flowerpot.", "I found a rusted hinge pin in the bottom of the corner, but I cannot recall whether it belonged to the gate or the old chest.", "A voice from the lane calls my name, but I cannot place it. For one breath it sounds like yesterday's voice; then it is only someone asking directions.", "There is a thin layer of dust on the porch, and three tiny flakes of rust near my corner.", "The broom handle is shorter than it was last week, and the missing piece must be somewhere along the wall.", "Three old seeds lie on the boards: one split, one smooth, and one blackened at one end.", "A shy traveler asks whether they may stay on the porch until the rain stops, and says they will not move anything.", "A stranger says they found a locked metal box in the yard and offers to open it in front of me.", "The visitor asks to sit close enough for warmth, but that spot is exactly where I keep the seed and the small tin cup.", "I find a bent but usable nail beside a shard of colored glass. Keeping both would mean opening my jar and rearranging everything inside.", "Under the third board I discover a tin capsule with unfamiliar handwriting, but lifting it means removing the board I use to mark each crack.", "A neighbor leaves a wool blanket folded at the threshold and says I may keep it for the cold.", "A young carpenter asks whether I need the straight nail from his pouch.", "A muddy stranger points out a small brass key buried beside the threshold and asks if I want to help investigate.", "A weary traveler asks if they may warm their hands around my little tin cup without touching anything else.", "A passerby says, “You keep everything, even broken things. That must be very wise.”", "The board under my left foot has begun to sink into wet dry rot.", "A sharp iron shard is cutting into my heel where I sit, but moving it means lifting the loose board above my seed tin.", "Rain blows through the broken side of the porch, but the only dry place for my seed tin is beside the crack I use to count the days.", "While rolling the half-eaten seed across my fingers, I let it drop into the dark gap beside the threshold and cannot see where it landed.", "The hinge catches my finger as I turn it, and the sharp pain makes me jerk back into the old iron basket.", "I mark today’s rain by scoring the fourth crack, then discover I have already scored it twice.", "I remember last night’s rain exactly until you ask where the lantern used to hang.", "A voice in the lane sounds like the one that used to call from the garden, but I cannot remember the gardener’s face.", "Someone moved my tin cup from the corner to the middle board, and now the cup catches rainwater instead of sitting dry.", "There is dust on the porch, but sweeping near my corner would also erase the light footprints in it.", "The kettle on the porch has just begun to whistle over a small fire.", "I have three nails beside the threshold and no immediate use for any of them.", "I bite the half-eaten seed and find it bitter, with a stale, smoky taste.", "Afternoon sun strikes the old iron scrap beside me, and the metal becomes too hot to touch.", "The rotten board dips beneath my heel, releasing a damp breath of wet wood and cold air.", "A wasp settles on my forearm while I am sorting a coil of copper wire in the porch corner.", "The bitter smell of old smoke reaches me from the lane, though there is no fire visible.", "A sharp-edged ceramic shard cuts the pad of my thumb as I test it against my teeth.", "A square of dry wood would raise my seat above the damp floor, but it would cover the faint ring left by an old jar.", "A stranger places a bright brass button on the floor near my threshold and asks me to inspect it.", "One porch board sags under a heavy iron kettle, and another sound board leads toward the dry corner.", "The bent nail I use to lift loose splinters snaps as I pry up a splinter from the threshold.", "A small clay cup slips from my hand and breaks beside the threshold.", "I try to pry open a rusted tin with my nail, and the lid cuts my hand before it gives.", "I remember a woman leaving three white beans on this step yesterday, but I cannot recall whether she wore a blue shawl or a gray one.", "Yesterday I hid a flat button beneath the loose third stone, and today I can picture the hiding place but not the button's color.", "I remember yesterday's wind, but not whether it came before or after the cart passed the lane.", "There are six loose screws on the step, and none of them has a purpose I can name.", "A dry leaf lands in my corner, perfectly intact, though it makes no useful shade or cover.", "I have swept the same small square of floor for ten minutes, and the dust keeps returning from beneath the nearest board.", "You sit on the far edge of the porch and ask whether you may stay for an hour.", "You say the porch is gloomy and offer to bring a lamp tomorrow.", "You hold out a wool scarf, saying you no longer need it, and the porch is colder than it was this morning.", "I find a sealed letter in the weeds beside the path, and you say it may contain news from the village.", "The kettle has boiled, and you ask if I would like you to pour a cup and sit beside me.", "A handful of rain-soaked leaves slap against the back of my neck while I am sitting in the porch corner.", "I lift a dusty copper wire from the floor and find one end still warm from the afternoon sun.", "The bitter smell of turnip greens drifts up from the lane, and my stomach clenches before I see any food.", "The rain has loosened a rusted washer from the third board. I can keep it in my tin, but I will have to move the seed cup first.", "A child waits in the rain and asks whether my dry corner can hold two people, though the tin cup and seed already fill the space.", "The loose gate hinge will swing quietly if I oil it, but doing so means taking the iron scrap I keep beside the threshold.", "While reaching into the jar, I knock the heavy iron scrap off the shelf and hear it strike the floorboards.", "I try to tell whether the seed in my palm is the split one from yesterday, but I cannot remember where I put it.", "My nail slips from the rusted tin lid and scores a bright line across my thumb.", "I remember the old tin lid had a crescent-shaped nick, but I cannot recall whether it was on the hinge side or the handle side.", "I know I hid the flat iron pin beneath the loose stone near the threshold, but I cannot remember which of the two loose stones it was.", "The hinge sounds exactly as it did on the morning the gardener left, but I cannot remember whether anyone else heard it that day.", "A stranger offers me a new brass button in exchange for letting them search under the threshold boards.", "The wind is sharp today, and a neighbor offers to lend me a thick coat for the afternoon.", "A stranger says there is an old chest buried beyond the fence and asks whether I want to help dig it up.", "A traveler standing outside the porch says, “You look like you’ve been expecting someone.”", "A neighbor leans against the porch rail and says, “You’ve made this dump into a proper little home.”", "The small tin cup beside me has a shallow dent in its rim, but it still holds water.", "A bent nail lies beside the threshold, and another piece of wire rests on the board above it.", "A shallow pan of rainwater sits beside the porch, reflecting the broken edge of the roof.", "I open a tin of old pepper flakes while sorting the porch scraps, and the dust shoots straight into my nose.", "A frost settles on the iron hinge, and I reach out to turn it just to hear whether the cold has loosened the pin.", "The old vinegar bottle leaks a sharp smell across the porch while I am counting the loose screws.", "The broken latch on my bucket can be straightened with a flat stone, but doing so would crush the small blue feather tucked beneath its rim.", "A dry square of cardboard would lift my seat above the damp patch, but it would cover the pale ring where my tin cup stood yesterday.", "A stranger places a rusted key at the edge of my corner and asks whether I know which lock it came from.", "A bird sang from the drainpipe this morning, but when I try to place the tune, it might have been yesterday afternoon or three mornings ago.", "I found a jar with a faded paper label last week, but I cannot remember whether the word on it was 'seed' or 'salt'.", "I remember someone crossing the porch in six steps, but today I cannot tell whether there were six steps or seven.", "A neighbor offers me a bowl of hot stew and says I may keep the empty bowl afterward.", "I could oil the gate hinge and end its creaking, but the oil would cover the rust pattern I have been watching for months.", "There is one good seed left in my palm, and the traveler across the step has not eaten since morning.", "I try to lift a rusted washer with the split seed held in my palm, and both shoot away from me.", "I step on the second board without testing it, and it sinks into the wet rot until my boot nearly reaches the mud.", "I tip my tin cup to refill it, and the water runs across the floorboards toward the crack I use to count the days.", "I have four buttons on the step, and only one of them has two holes.", "A strand of frayed cord lies beside the threshold, and I need to mend the loop on my seed tin.", "The broom has left a neat fan of dust beside the bucket, but the final stroke would erase one small footprint in it.", "You ask why I keep a cracked cup when a sound one would hold more water.", "You tell me the porch looks lonely and offer to bring company by the fire tomorrow."]

